{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1515171047,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyODYzNDMzNTQ=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2061","origin":"github"},"title":"RF datalad ls output to use pyout","message":"#### What is the problem?\n\nI like what I did in `datalad ls` for formatting of the output while listing datasets and s3:// urls.  Really handy and colorful and separated nicely out the formatting (via obscure hacks on top of python's `format`) and content (class instance objects being queried with lazy evaluation of the attributes, so that no length querying of annex is done unless some attribute among those is requested).\nBut, for the niceman project, @kyleam is working on the  https://github.com/pyout/pyout - an output helper where style, content, and actual control are three separate things, with the control part allowing for asynchronous querying (callbacks provided as the value).  It would be nice if datalad started to use pyout, and it could also serve as a good test use-case for the pyout to enrich or adjust its API etc to fit the datalad's usecase\n\nThis also will resolve outstanding user's FAQ: what those columns mean ;)\nFrom my memory necessary tune ups/tricky points might me (mark \"checked\" whenever that functionality is already provided, might be moved into a corresponding PR):\n- [x] IIRC ATM datalad formats via attributes of instances, not dicts. So ideally pyout would support consuming records as objects to getattr, not only dicts\n- [ ] ATM sizes are reported in a single column -- would need to be split\n- [ ] would need to use humanize (related in pyout: https://github.com/pyout/pyout/issues/23 for sizes reporting)\n- [ ] some attributes are lazy (e.g. sizes) and there is multiple of them... that is why I was suggesting in pyout to be able to specify a single callback for multiple fields","files":null}]}